<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<section xml:id="sec-gas3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Combined gas law</title>

	<p>
		Since pressure and volume are inversely proportional
		<me>
			P \times V = c
		</me>
		volume and temperature are directly proportional
		<me>
			\frac{V}{T} = c
		</me>
		and pressure and temperature are directly proportional
		<me>
			\frac{P}{T} = c
		</me>
		we can describe these three relationships with a combined equation:
		<me>
			\frac{P\times V}{T}=c
		</me>
		Since this relationship T should hold under multiple conditions (assuming that
		the amount of gas doesn’t change), we can derive the combined gas law:
		<me>
			\frac{P_1\times V_1}{T_1}=\frac{P_2\times V_2}{T_2}
		</me>
	</p>

	<exercises>
		<title>Combined Gas Law Exercises</title>

		<exercisegroup>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					Combined gas law (the following problems assume that the amount of gas
					doesn’t change).
				</p>

				<p>
					If a gas has a pressure of 17.0 atm, a volume of 1.05 L and a temperature
					of 302 K, what is...
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						the new pressure if the volume is decreased to 0.55 L and the temperature
						is increased to 331 K?
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						36 atm
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						the new volume if the pressure is increased to 34.0 atm and the
						temperature decreases to 151 K?
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						0.263 L
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						the new temperature if the pressure increases to 33.5 atm and the volume
						increases to 7.55 L?
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						4280 K
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>
		</exercisegroup>

		<exercise>
			<statement>
				<p>
					If a gas has a pressure of 1.52 MPa, a volume of 223 L and a temperature of
					<m>151 ^oC</m>, what is the new temperature if the pressure decreases to 453 kPa
					and the volume increases to 1.55 kL?
				</p>
			</statement>

			<answer>
				<p>
					879K
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>
	</exercises>
</section>